-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
-- P32 · 0039 — get_public_role_user_ids(_role text)
--
--            ⚠⚠  PREPARED ONLY. NO MIGRATION IS AUTHORIZED.  ⚠⚠
--            ⚠⚠  ORDINAL 0039 IS RESERVED AND **NOT CONSUMED**.  ⚠⚠
--
-- ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
-- WHY THIS FILE IS NOT IN supabase/migrations/ — AND WHY THAT IS NOT PEDANTRY
--
-- `apply-migration.yml`'s allowlist admits **every `.sql` file under
-- `supabase/migrations/`**. It does not read comments. A file sitting there
-- with "NOT AUTHORIZED" in its header is not protected by that header; it is
-- one dispatch away from running.
--
-- **A comment is not a control. The path is the control.**
--
-- This is not a hypothetical in this unit. The withdrawn `0029` revoked this
-- exact object without Owner Decision 6, reached `staging` inside PR #274, and
-- sat in the dispatchable directory until R-8 renamed it `UNAPPLIED_`. That is
-- the #272 bootstrap hazard, demonstrated on this very function. Keeping `0039`
-- outside `supabase/migrations/` is the lesson applied rather than restated.
--
-- There is deliberately NO rollback file: no migration exists to roll back.
--
-- ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
-- 1 · THIS OBJECT IS IN NEITHER SET B NOR SET C
--
-- Measured on staging fpszggreishhuvdpkmdr, SELECT only, 2026-09-22:
--
--   proname      get_public_role_user_ids(text)
--   prosecdef    true
--   provolatile  's'        ← **STABLE, not VOLATILE**
--   proacl       =X/postgres | postgres=X/postgres | anon=X/postgres |
--                authenticated=X/postgres | service_role=X/postgres
--
-- Sets B and C are both defined by `provolatile = 'v'`. This function is
-- `STABLE`, so it falls outside both, and the Auditor's R-8 allocation records
-- it as "neither". Measured, not assumed.
--
-- It is still `SECURITY DEFINER`, still `PUBLIC`-granted, and still an
-- enumeration surface. Being outside Sets B and C says which population it
-- belongs to. It does not say it is safe.
--
-- ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
-- 2 · THE WITHDRAWAL OF 0029 RESTORED IT TO ITS PRE-#274 STATE
--
-- Merged `0029` carried:
--
--   REVOKE ALL ON FUNCTION public.get_public_role_user_ids(text) FROM public;
--   REVOKE ALL ON FUNCTION public.get_public_role_user_ids(text) FROM anon;
--   GRANT EXECUTE ON FUNCTION public.get_public_role_user_ids(text) TO authenticated, service_role;
--
-- It was never dispatched (`schema_migrations`: 8 rows, latest
-- `20260915130151`, 2026-09-22T08:42Z), and R-8 renamed the file `UNAPPLIED_`.
-- So no closure of this object has ever run on either lane, and none is
-- authorised.
--
-- ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
-- 3 · THE CALLER BREAKAGE — WHY OWNER DECISION 6 IS STILL OUTSTANDING
--
-- Each link re-verified in this tree, 2026-09-22:
--
--   src/lib/adminBrand.ts:35          getAdminIds() → rpc("get_public_role_user_ids", {_role:"admin"})
--   src/components/AutoBadge.tsx:70   queryFn: getAdminIds      ("One shared key for the whole app")
--   src/components/UserIdentityBlock.tsx:212   renders <AutoBadge userId=… />
--   src/App.tsx:395                   /profile/:userId declared OUTSIDE RequireAuth
--
-- `UserIdentityBlock` also renders on `Index.tsx` (the landing page),
-- `Winners.tsx`, `PublicProfile.tsx`, `FeaturedArtistPage.tsx` and
-- `JournalArticle.tsx` — none of which redirects a signed-out visitor.
--
-- **The failure would be silent by construction.** `adminBrand.ts:35`
-- destructures `{ data }` and never inspects `error`. A 42501 yields
-- `data = null` → an empty `Set` → `resolveName()` returns the original name.
-- The file's own comment states the consequence: the admin's brand name and
-- verified badge revert "for every non-admin viewer". No error, no console
-- warning, no failing test.
--
-- So after a revoke, a SIGNED-OUT visitor to the landing page sees the admin's
-- raw profile name and no verified tick, while a SIGNED-IN visitor sees the
-- brand name and the tick.
--
-- **That is a product question, not a technical one:** *should the admin brand
-- name and verified badge be visible to signed-out visitors?* It belongs to the
-- Owner. It is **Owner Decision 6** and it is **outstanding**.
--
-- The body is not the issue — it already allow-lists in-body to admin/judge and
-- raises `42501` otherwise (`P1-revocation-list.md` §2.4, re-verified
-- 2026-09-21). A grant closure here is defence in depth, and it is the defence
-- in depth that changes what a visitor sees.
--
-- ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
-- 4 · STATUS
--
--   ordinal 0039              RESERVED, NOT CONSUMED
--   migration file            DOES NOT EXIST, by instruction
--   rollback file             DOES NOT EXIST — nothing to roll back
--   authorised for dispatch   NO
--   blocked by                Owner Decision 6
--   set membership            neither B nor C (STABLE)
--   current lane state        unchanged from pre-#274, on both lanes
--
-- If Owner Decision 6 is ever ruled in favour of closing it, whoever writes the
-- migration should take `0039` — it is held for exactly that — write the
-- rollback in the same PR, and apply the two-lane PUBLIC discipline used by
-- `0038`: staging holds PUBLIC, production does not, and the rollback must
-- never recreate it.
--
-- No SQL statement appears below this line, and that is the point.
